This isn't a game so much as a story. Sure, "walking simulator" is the pejorative some like to sling, but that misses the point. It's about discovery. Discovery of the story, the history, the characters. So if you come at in from that angle you'll probably enjoy the ride.  For such a small team the developers deserve much credit for creating a very polished game. The graphics, textures, animation, shader effects, and sound design are comparable with much larger outfits. However it's not without its weaknesses. The puzzles are a mixed bag. Most are "find the missing piece" or "match the picture". But some had me searching for ever for that less-than-obvious missing piece. One in particular had a pretty obscure solution that I only managed to "solve" by intuition and luck. And another felt like they had designed a bigger puzzle but then turned it into a simple "missing piece".
decent story-telling puzzler with a few rough patches. It's short (about 8 hrs for me, and I explore
). But well done for an indie game.  I got it on sale, and I recommend you wait for one too.

I've been playing this for almost 17 hours,just finished it and want to point out what a masterpiece of a game!
It has everything:
- superb storytelling with lot of action packed sequences as if you are in a thriller - horror movie;
- addictive gameplay with a lot of original ideas implemented with style in the story;
- great graphics, with realistic detailed backgrounds, people and weather effects;
- superb soundtrack, some rock hits for the fans;
- a lot of easter eggs,collectibles and background story files like manuscripts etc.
Also they added free downloadable bonus material like making-of videos, artwork, game scripts, so you can see what an incredible Alan Wake world Remedy managed to create.
I would recommend this game to all gamers out there, it's definitely a classic! I'm really sorry that they wouldn't make a sequel though, and only the American Nightmare one.
Thanks for reading!  10.01.2014

do
play this without playing The Banner Saga 1 first. The story is one of the strongest suits of this series, and it continues from where you left it in the first installment. You import your TBS 1 save before starting this one, so the choices you made, the characters you built, your items, etc. remain with you.
Ok, so I'm going to assume you've played the first TBS and now you're considering if you should continue with the series or not. The short answer is: if you liked the first one, yes, definitely. And if you didn't... you should get checked, because there's probably something wrong with you.
That should do it for most readers, but maybe you're somewhere in between, torn in your opinion about the first game. In that case, you should know:
- In TBS2, the story, art, soundtrack, all the mood of the game remains on the same standard as the first's, with a few more cutscenes;
- They added Talents to your attributes, so now you can choose 1 of 2 special attributes to level after you master the basic attributes (2 different Talents available for each attribute);
- There are new classes of playable characters and enemies, the most interesting ones in the game being among these;
- Level cap is now 10, and each character can choose one out of 1-5 new skills to have as his second one once they reach lvl 6.
Put simply, TBS 2 improves upon pretty much everything compared to the first one, while losing nothing of what was already perfect. So... yeah, buy it, and join the prayers asking for the 3rd one to come out ASAP, because I wanna see how this thing ends.

